Let go of what every statistics read made: its callback and the arrays it hands across

Every getStats kept about 2 KB for good. WebRTCStatsCollectorCallback::Create took one
reference more than WebRTC gives back, so the callback stayed with its two closures and
whatever their caller captured in them (the plugin's answer to Dart). And
portable::vector, the arrays handed across the library's border (the reports, the
members of each, lists of devices, senders, receivers, codecs...), destroyed its
elements but never freed its array; its copy assignment lost the old array, or left
elements alive past the new size, a copy of an empty one held an undefined pointer, and
a vector moved from with move() was left holding the array too.

A vector's array is now made and freed by the library alone (allocate_array,
free_array; on Windows the library and its users may each have a heap of their own),
its elements constructed in it and destroyed by whoever holds the vector; its layout is
the same. It copies, assigns (itself too) and moves without losing an array or an
element, and copies an array it is given instead of taking it over (portable::map, the
one user, built on it). A string assigned itself keeps its text. Tested by
test/portable_vector.test.cc and test/stats_callback.test.cc.

